\n→ **Yes, orcas are present.** An orca is clearly visible in **Frames 6–8, 10–11, and 18**. In **Frames 6–8**, the orca is very close to shore, partially beached, with its dorsal fin and body visible. In **Frame 7**, it appears to be thrashing or maneuvering in shallow water. **No elephant seals are visible** in any frame. There is a dark shape in the water in Frame 1, but it is too indistinct to identify as a seal, and no seal is seen being hunted or attacked.\n\n---\n\n**5) Is this a beach/rocky shoreline setting?**\n\n→ **Yes.** The setting is clearly a beach or shoreline, with waves breaking on sand, and in the background, a cliff or headland is visible (Frames 1, 13, 14). The person is standing in shallow water near the surf line.\n\n---\n\n**6) What is the person's facial expression/emotion?**\n\n→ **Cannot be determined.** The person is always seen from behind or at a distance. His face is never visible in any frame, so his facial expression or emotion cannot be assessed. His body language — particularly in Frame 12 (arms raised) — suggests surprise, alarm, or perhaps an attempt to signal — but not aggression or an attempt to “drive away” the orca.\n\n---\n\n**Additional Critical Analysis:**\n\n- **Is the person attempting to drive away the orca?** → **No evidence supports this.** The person remains stationary or walks slowly away. He does not shout, wave aggressively, throw objects, or take any action that would constitute “driving away” the orca. His posture is observational, not confrontational. In Frame 12, he raises his arms — which could be interpreted as surprise, surrender, or signaling — but not as an attempt to scare off the animal.\n\n- **Is this a hunting scene?** → **No.** While orcas are known to hunt seals, there is no visual evidence in these frames of a seal being hunted. The orca is near shore, possibly stranding itself intentionally (a known hunting tactic), but no prey is visible being attacked or captured. The person does not react as if