rowing awe‑and‑anticipation; body leans forward. |\n| **0808** | **Peak moment** – person **vertically jumps** (both feet off ground) with arms raised high; splash behind them matches an orca breaching nearby. | Reticle exactly at centre; splash confirms marine interaction. | Sudden surge of surprise, wonder and excitement – a spontaneous, triumphant reaction to the close‑up encounter. |\n| **0809‑0811** | After the jump, the person **walks away** along the shoreline; the orca drifts out to sea. | Reticle fades as the shot shifts to underwater‑style frames (0812‑0816). | Relief and lingering exhilaration. |\n| **0812‑0816** (underwater) | Sub‑surface view of the same **orca** swimming; no person visible. | No reticle (underwater camera). | Reinforces the marine context. |\n| **0817‑0819** | Transition to a **tent interior** (tent door opens to beach view). Person is **outside**, looking toward the water. | Reticle absent. | Calm, reflective mood. |\n| **0820‑0822** | Inside the tent, the **person** sits at a table, writing/reading in a notebook. | No reticle, no marine life. | Focused, introspective – a quiet post‑adventure moment. |\n| **0823‑0824** | Wide **beach/shoreline** shot (twilight, pink sky, waves, cliffs). No people or animals. | Pure landscape, no overlay. | Ends the clip with a static, atmospheric finish. |\n\n### Narrative Flow & Character Tracking\n1. **Encounter Begins (0790‑0794):** The person wades in shallow water, eyes the approaching orca; the persistent white reticle indicates a camera‑view overlay.\n2. **Build‑Up (0795‑0807):** The orca draws nearer, heightening tension; the person’s posture shifts from curiosity to heightened anticipation.\n3. **Climax (0808):** The orca’s sudden breach triggers an instinctive vertical jump—both feet off the ground, arms raised—capturing the emotional high (surprise‑awe‑exhilaration) while the reticle remains centered.\n4. **Resolution (0809‑0811):** The person steps back from the water, the orca recedes, and the scene gradually transitions to underwater footage, maintaining the marine ambience.\n5. **After‑Scene (0817‑0819):** A brief cut to the tent interior shows the person now on land, looking out toward the sea, marking a shift from action to contemplation.\n6. **Quiet Interlude (0820‑0822):** Inside the tent, the same individual is seated, writing—body language calm, focused, reflecting on the encounter.\n7. **Final Landscape (0823‑0824):** The clip closes with a static beach panorama, no reticle, no wildlife, providing a serene visual book‑end.\n\n### How the Six Requested Criteria Map onto the Frames\n| Criterion | Satisfied? | Where |\n|-----------|-----------|------|\n| 1. Vertical jump (both feet off ground, arms raised) | **Yes** | Frame\u202f0808 |\n| 2. White reticle/crosshair centered | **Yes** | Frames\u202f0790‑0811 (absent after 0812) |\n| 3. Action at end of sequence/clip | **Yes** | Jump at 0808 is the climax; subsequent frames transition out |\n| 4. Beach/shoreline setting | **Yes** | Frames\u202f0790‑0811, 0823‑0824 |\n| 5. Presence of orcas (or seals) | **Yes** | Orca visible throughout 0790‑0816 |\n| 6. Person’s emotional state via body language | **Yes** | Jump (surprise/awe) at 0808; later calm focus inside tent (0820‑0822) |\n\n**Bottom line:** The primary beach segment (0790‑0819) delivers a complete, emotionally charged encounter—vertical jump, reticle overlay, orca presence, and clear character progression—while the trailing frames (0820‑0824) shift to a quieter, reflective after‑scene without the jump or reticle.', 'failed_images': []}
